System error codes and where they come from.

`ca::errc` names the errno values ca uses as `ca::error` constants, written per platform in
`errc.hpp` and checked against `<errno.h>` when the library builds, so no program needs the
header: `errc::no_entry` (ENOENT), `errc::access_denied` (EACCES), `errc::exists`,
`errc::invalid_argument`, `errc::no_space`, `errc::out_of_range`, `errc::try_again`,
`errc::interrupted`, `errc::not_supported`, `errc::not_recoverable` (what a trapped context
fails with), and the rest of the common set.

| Function | Answers |
|---|---|
| `sys::error_name(e)` | the errc constant's name, `"no_entry"`; empty for a code with none |
| `sys::error_message(e)` | the system's text, `"No such file or directory"`, untranslated |
| `sys::last_error()` | the calling thread's last system error |

Both strings have static storage. `error_message` never fails: an unknown code gives
`"Unknown error"`.

```c++
#include "ca/error.hpp"
#include "ca/file.hpp"
#include "ca/sv.hpp"

[[nodiscard]] static auto describe(const ca::error e) noexcept -> ca::sv::str {
	if (e == ca::errc::no_entry)
		return "missing";
	if (e == ca::errc::access_denied || e == ca::errc::not_permitted)
		return "forbidden";
	return ca::sys::error_message(e);
}

auto main(void) -> int {
	if (describe(ca::errc::no_entry) != ca::sv::str{"missing"})
		return 1;
	if (ca::sys::error_name(ca::errc::exists) != ca::sv::str{"exists"})
		return 2;
	auto gone = ca::sys::rmdir("/definitely/not/here");
	return !gone && ca::sys::last_error() == gone.error() ? 0 : 3;
}
```

### ca owns errno

`sys::last_error()` is ca's own thread-local errno, not libc's: every failing `ca::syscall`
call stores its code there (one store relative to `%fs`, no call), and the few paths that still
go through libc copy libc's errno into it (`impl::libc_error`, kept in its own object so that
programs without those paths never link errno). Since only failures write it, a successful
call never clobbers the error a caller is about to inspect. `sys::set_last_error(e)` sets it
by hand.
